Ladok, the pacifist-to-be gnomish healer saw more action during his first 
ten turns in the dungeon than most characters get on the entire first 
level. Here's how it happened.

There was this fountain right next to the stairs and I decided to have a 
sip as I usually do that this early in the game, especially when already 
poison resistant (you never know when it'll earn you a wish).

It was as easy as quaff to unleash the water demon I was looking for, but 
sadly he wasn't in a mood for handing out wishes, and worse yet, he seemed 
to hold some sort of old grudge against gnomes. I recognized the option to 
stick my tail between my legs and quietly skulk back upstairs to the world 
outside and end my pitiful search for the amulet at turn 4, but for some 
reason I decided against it.

A few turns on the retreat later, during which I got confirmed my 
suspicion that my brave pet dog indeed would not lift a finger to help his 
master against such a formidable foe, and I ended up a cornered 
scalpel-wielding level 1 healer with nothing but starting equipment 
against this rather upset water demon. I had started pondering what to 
busy myself with during the last two-or-so turns in my life when I 
realised that my potions of extra healing had a somewhat smoky hue to them 
and that one of the four in fact seemed to be blessed by the gods! I 
shrugged, muttered a "why the heck not" and quaffed the thing, and to my 
surprise one of those bottle-dwelling genies actually made an appearance 
to show me that fountain demons aren't the guys to ask for wishes in this 
dungeon.

So what did I want from this unexpected wish? Something to save my life 
from that still rampaging demon certainly, but wouldn't it be fun if 
whatever I chose also was a bit useful after having saved me from the 
imminent danger? What single item is capable of doing that, especially in 
the hands of a level 1 character? No weapon or regular piece of armor 
would do the trick, nor even any artifact I could think of. A wand of 
death wouldn't harm a demon, and how much fun is it to use a wish for a 
wand of teleport or digging?

I pretty much narrowed the choices down to speed boots (maybe I could 
manage to put them on and edge past him if I quaffed healing potions every 
other turn or so?), scrolls of taming or genocide (but wasn't quite sure 
that either would work with a demon), wand of polymorph (could be fun with 
that cowardly dog afterwards, but a pretty uncertain way to survive the 
demon) or a blessed figurine of something nasty. In the end I went with 
the figurine idea and made it an archon, hoping that I'd survive long 
enough to apply it and let it do its stuff. Considering the strangeness 
I'd seen so far I more or less expected the archon to come out hostile.

The demon brought me down to 6 hitpoints during the turn I used to make my 
wish, so I didn't dare applying the figurine yet since I had no idea who'd 
get the first attack. I spent three turns quaffing my remaining extra 
healing potions until I had 10 hitpoints at the start of my turn and then 
decided to go for it. Luckily the creature that emerged seemed peaceful 
enough and made short work of the water demon in two turns.

I guess it was just another coincidence that the archon came equipped with 
Demonbane... Ah well, fun to have survived it and now for some more 
dungeon exploring.

Would you have made some other wish?

/Per W.

